window.EditableMathsPilot=Object.assign(window.EditableMathsPilot||{},{"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-FREE-Q09":{"question":"<p>The increasing function f is graphed with rectangles. Values are:</p><table><tr><th>x</th><th>0</th><th>0.5</th><th>1</th><th>1.5</th><th>2</th><th>2.5</th><th>3</th></tr><tr><td>f(x)</td><td>20</td><td>21</td><td>24</td><td>29</td><td>36</td><td>45</td><td>56</td></tr></table><p></p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Use the rectangle areas to demonstrate and explain 32.5 &lt; ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>1.5</sup>f(x)dx &lt; 37.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use the table to determine the best estimate possible for ∫<sub>1</sub><sup>3</sup>f(x)dx.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>State two ways to obtain a more accurate value.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Left sum 0.5(20 + 21 + 24) = 32.5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Right sum 0.5(21 + 24 + 29) = 37.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Since f is increasing, left rectangles underestimate and right rectangles overestimate.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Lower sum = 0.5(24 + 29 + 36 + 45) = 67.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Upper sum = 0.5(29 + 36 + 45 + 56) = 83.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Trapezoidal estimate (67 + 83)/2 = 75.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Use narrower intervals and more function values.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>If the defining rule is known, integrate it exactly.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>Flight lateness T is uniform on [−30, 60] minutes, with mean 15 and variance 675. A sample of 30 arrival times gives sample mean T̄.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine P(10 ≤ T̄ ≤ 20) to two decimal places.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Sketch the likely distribution of sample means from many samples of 30, referring to the probability in (a).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>By the central limit theorem T̄ is approximately N(15, 675/30).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Standard deviation √22.5 ≈ 4.743.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P(10 ≤ T̄ ≤ 20) ≈ 0.71.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Bell-shaped normal approximation centred at 15.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Indicates standard deviation about 4.743 minutes.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Shades 10 to 20 with area approximately 0.71.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2021-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>Let f(x) = x<sup>2</sup>e<sup>x</sup>.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show f′(x) = xe<sup>x</sup>(x + 2).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use calculus to find and classify all stationary points.</p>","marks":7,"lines":21,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine all points of inflection.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Sketch f, indicating all stationary points and inflection points.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Product rule gives 2xe<sup>x</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>e<sup>x</sup>; factorises to the required expression.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Sets xe<sup>x</sup>(x + 2) = 0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Obtains x = 0 and x = −2, since e<sup>x</sup> is never zero.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Obtains f″(x) = e<sup>x</sup>(x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 2).</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>At x = −2, f″ &lt; 0, so (−2, 4/e<sup>2</sup>) is a local maximum.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>At x = 0, f″ &gt; 0, so (0, 0) is a local minimum.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Correct stationary coordinates and classifications.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Solves f″ = 0: x = −2 ± √2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Coordinates approximately (−3.414, 0.384) and (−0.586, 0.191), with concavity changes.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows local maximum.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows local minimum.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows both inflection points.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Correct overall shape, approaching y = 0 as x → −∞ and rising as x → ∞.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":15,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2016-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>A particle has r(t) = (4cos 2t, 2cos t) cm, with time in seconds. Its path is shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Express the path as a Cartesian equation.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find speed to 0.01 cm/second when the particle first reaches x = −2.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Write a trigonometric definite integral for distance from A to B. Do not evaluate.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses cos 2t = 2cos<sup>2</sup>t − 1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Eliminates t to obtain x = 2y<sup>2</sup> − 4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Restricts −2 ≤ y ≤ 2, equivalently −4 ≤ x ≤ 4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>First x = −2 occurs at t = π/3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>v(t) = (−8sin 2t, −2sin t).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses speed √(64sin<sup>2</sup>2t + 4sin<sup>2</sup>t).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Speed = √51 ≈ 7.14 cm/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>A is reached at t = 0 and B at t = π.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses magnitude of velocity.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Distance ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>π</sup>√(64sin<sup>2</sup>2t + 4sin<sup>2</sup>t)dt.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2024-FREE-Q06":{"question":"","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Solve x + y + z = 4; x − y − z = 2; 2x − 3y + z = 11.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Replace the third equation by 2x − ky + z = 11. Correct reduction gives (k + 1)y = −4. Find k for which there is no unique solution.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>For that k, explain the geometry of the three planes.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Adding the first two equations gives x = 3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses y + z = 1 and −3y + z = 5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Solution (x, y, z) = (3, −1, 2).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>k = −1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>No common intersection exists.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>The planes are not parallel; pairwise intersections are distinct parallel lines.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2024-FREE-Q06":{"question":"<p>The graph y = x sin(πx), 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, is shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Shade the region whose area is ∫<sub>1/6</sub><sup>1/2</sup>x sin(πx)dx.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Using the shown rectangle areas, demonstrate and explain (1 + 2√3)/72 &lt; ∫<sub>1/6</sub><sup>1/2</sup>x sin(πx)dx &lt; (3 + √3)/36.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Suggest one way to improve the approximation.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Shades under the curve above the x-axis between x = 1/6 and x = 1/2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Lower rectangle sum (1/6)[1/12 + √3/6] = (1 + 2√3)/72.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Upper sum (1/6)[√3/6 + 1/2] = (3 + √3)/36.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Explains that increasing f makes left rectangles lower and right rectangles upper bounds.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Uses more rectangles of smaller width, or averages upper and lower sums.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":5,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2016-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>The circle x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 16 has horizontal intercepts A and B. DC is tangent at D(2, −2√3) and meets the x-axis at C.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show that the tangent is √3y = x − 8.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine C.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>The region bounded by arc AD, segment DC and the x-axis rotates about the x-axis. Determine volume to 0.01 cubic units.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Implicit derivative y′ = −x/y.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>At D the slope is 1/√3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Point-slope form simplifies to √3y = x − 8.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>C = (8, 0).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Splits the solid at x = 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Sets V = π∫<sub>−4</sub><sup>2</sup>(16 − x<sup>2</sup>)dx + π∫<sub>2</sub><sup>8</sup>(x − 8)<sup>2</sup>/3 dx.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Evaluates to 96π.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Volume ≈ 301.59 cubic units.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>Drug concentration t hours after a dose is C = 4e<sup>−0.05t</sup> mg/L. A patient given a dose at 9 am enters crisis if concentration falls below 2.5 mg/L.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find concentration immediately after the dose.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find concentration at 11.30 am.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find the rate of change at 1 pm.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the latest time for another dose to avoid crisis.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>4 mg/L.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses t = 2.5 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>C ≈ 3.53 mg/L.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>C′ = −0.2e<sup>−0.05t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>At t = 4, C′ ≈ −0.164 mg/L/hour.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Solves 2.5 = 4e<sup>−0.05t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>t ≈ 9.4001 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Latest time approximately 6.24 pm.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2020-FREE-Q06":{"question":"<p>Let f(x) = ln x. A vertical translation is g(x) = f(x) + a, a vertical dilation is h(x) = cf(x), and a horizontal dilation is p(x) = f(bx) with scale 1/b.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Express g(x) = ln(4x) as a vertical translation of f, stating the amount.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Express h(x) = ln(√x) as a vertical dilation of f, stating its scale.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Express p(x) = ln x + 4 as a horizontal dilation of f, stating its scale.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>ln(4x) = ln x + ln 4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Translation upward ln 4 units.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>ln(√x) = ½ln x = ½f(x).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Vertical scale factor ½.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Writes 4 = ln(e<sup>4</sup>).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>p(x) = ln(e<sup>4</sup>x) = f(e<sup>4</sup>x).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Horizontal scale factor e<sup>−4</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Sketch |z − 2i| + |z − (3 − 2i)| = 5.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Describe (z + i)conj(z + i) = 2, where conj denotes complex conjugation.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Write complex equations or inequalities for the indicated arc, without using Re(z) or Im(z).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Shows endpoints (0, 2) and (3, −2).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Joins them by the included straight line segment, since their separation is 5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Rewrites |z + i|<sup>2</sup> = 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Circle with radius √2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Centre −i.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Circle equation |z − (1 + i)| = 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Restricts Arg relative to centre.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>−π/2 ≤ Arg(z − (1 + i)) ≤ π, including endpoints.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>The curve x<sup>3</sup> − x<sup>2</sup> − 5x = 3 − y<sup>2</sup> and line y = √24 are shown. Their intersections satisfy x<sup>3</sup> − x<sup>2</sup> − 5x + 21 = 0.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Explain from the graph why the cubic has one real root and two conjugate non-real roots.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine the two exact non-real roots.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Calculate the shaded loop area to 0.001 square units.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>The horizontal line intersects the curve at exactly one real point.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>A real-coefficient cubic has three roots, with non-real roots occurring as conjugate pairs.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Factors as (x + 3)(x<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 7).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Non-real roots 2 ± i√3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses symmetry about x-axis and bounds −1 to 3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Upper branch y = √(3 − x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + 5x).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area = 2∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>3</sup>√(3 − x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + 5x)dx.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area = 256/15 ≈ 17.067 square units.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2023-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A vase is formed by revolving x = √[y(y<sup>2</sup> − 11y + 35)], 0 ≤ y ≤ 7, about the y-axis. All dimensions are centimetres.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Give an integral in h for water volume to depth h.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Water enters at 50 cm<sup>3</sup>/second. Find the time to fill the empty vase to 6 cm.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>At depth 6 cm another 30 cm<sup>3</sup> is added. Use increments to estimate the change in depth.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses disks πx<sup>2</sup>dy.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>V(h) = π∫<sub>0</sub><sup>h</sup>y(y<sup>2</sup> − 11y + 35)dy.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>V(6) = 162π ≈ 508.938 cm<sup>3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Time = 162π/50 ≈ 10.179 seconds.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>By FTC V′(h) = πh(h<sup>2</sup> − 11h + 35).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>V′(6) = 30π.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Δh ≈ ΔV/V′(6) = 1/π ≈ 0.318 cm.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-FREE-Q01":{"question":"<p>A bag contains one red and four green marbles. One is drawn; Y is the number of green marbles drawn.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete the distribution.</p><table><tr><th>y</th><th>0</th><th>1</th></tr><tr><td>P(Y = y)</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table><p></p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>State the distribution of Y.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine its mean and standard deviation.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>The draw is repeated five times with replacement. X is the total number of green marbles. State its distribution and parameters.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Find the probability of exactly two green marbles.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P(Y = 0) = 1/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P(Y = 1) = 4/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Bernoulli with success probability 4/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Mean = 4/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Standard deviation √[(4/5)(1/5)] = 2/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>X is binomial.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>X ~ Bin(5, 4/5).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Uses C(5,2)(4/5)<sup>2</sup>(1/5)<sup>3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Probability = 32/625 = 0.0512.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. A player wins $20 if the sum is less than five, $10 if greater than eight, and nothing otherwise.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete the table.</p><table><tr><th>Amount won</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr><tr><td>Probability</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table><p></p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine expected winnings per play.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Ravi charges Liu Yang $5 per play. Who is better off in the long term? Explain.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>What should Ravi charge to earn an expected profit equal to 20% of the charge?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Amounts $20, $10, $0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Probabilities 6/36, 10/36, 20/36 respectively.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>E(W) = 20(6/36) + 10(10/36).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Expected winnings = 55/9 ≈ $6.11.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Player’s expected net return = 55/9 − 5 = 10/9 dollars.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Thus Liu Yang gains about $1.11 per game in the long term.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Ravi loses that amount on average.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>For charge P, expected profit P − 55/9 = 0.2P.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>P = 275/36 ≈ $7.64.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2016-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>A lift’s velocity graph is shown. Maximum upward speed is 1.2 m/second. For the first 4 seconds acceleration is a(t) = kt; for the last 4 seconds it decelerates symmetrically. The ascent lasts 20 seconds.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show k = 3/20.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use increments to estimate the change in velocity from t = 2 to t = 2.1 seconds.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine the total ascent distance to 0.1 m.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Integrates acceleration using v(0) = 0: v(t) = kt<sup>2</sup>/2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>v(4) = 8k = 1.2, so k = 3/20.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses Δv ≈ a(2) × 0.1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Δv ≈ (3/20)(2)(0.1) = 0.03 m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses total area under velocity graph.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area = 2∫<sub>0</sub><sup>4</sup>3t<sup>2</sup>/40 dt + (16 − 4)(1.2).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Distance = 17.6 m.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2021-FREE-Q07":{"question":"<p>The function f(x) = 1/x is graphed on the supplied axes.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Shade two different regions, one on each graph, with area exactly ln 2.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Given ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup>(1/x)dx = ln 3, state the relationship between a and b.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Use the shown rectangles to demonstrate and explain 11/30 &lt; ∫<sub>2</sub><sup>3</sup>(1/x)dx &lt; 9/20.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Hence show 11/30 &lt; ln(1.5) &lt; 9/20.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>For example shade under y = 1/x from x = 1 to 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>A second example is from x = 2 to 4; any valid interval [a, 2a], a &gt; 0, is acceptable.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Integral = ln b − ln a = ln(b/a).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Thus b = 3a (for positive a and b).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Right rectangles give ½(2/5 + 1/3) = 11/30.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Left rectangles give ½(1/2 + 2/5) = 9/20.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>The function decreases, so the right sum is lower and the left sum upper.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Integrates [ln x]<sub>2</sub><sup>3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Integral = ln 3 − ln 2 = ln(1.5), giving the stated bounds.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2020-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>A council consultant estimates the proportion of residents who use the library.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the minimum sample size for a 95% confidence interval with margin of error 0.01.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>If sample size is limited to 500, find the maximum margin of error for a 99% confidence interval.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>The consultant samples passers-by outside the library at lunchtime. Identify and explain two possible biases.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses worst-case proportion p = 0.5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>n ≥ (1.96)<sup>2</sup>(0.25)/(0.01)<sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Minimum n = 9604.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses z ≈ 2.576 for 99%.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses p = 0.5 and n = 500.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Maximum error = 2.576√(0.25/500) ≈ 0.0576, or 5.76 percentage points.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Location bias: library visitors are more likely to be sampled.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Time bias: people available at lunchtime may differ from other residents, such as those working elsewhere.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>A magnetic field’s iron-filings slope is dy/dx = 1/(2x − 2), x ≠ 1. A force line through A(0, 1) is shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine the slope at A.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Explain the filings’ orientation at x = 1.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine the force-line equation through A.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Substitutes x = 0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Slope = −½.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Slope is undefined/infinite, so filings are vertical.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integrates y = ½ln|2x − 2| + C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses A(0, 1) to find C = 1 − ½ln 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Simplifies y = 1 + ½ln|x − 1|.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>For the branch through A, x &lt; 1, so y = 1 + ½ln(1 − x).</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2021-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>Complex numbers z₁ and z₂ are shown on the Argand plane.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Write z₁ in exact polar form.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Write z₁ in exact Cartesian form.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Plot iz₂ on the given diagram.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Describe the shaded locus in terms of z without Re(z) or Im(z). Its upper boundary is a circular arc centred at i.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Modulus 2 and argument 5π/6; z₁ = 2cis(5π/6).</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>z₁ = −√3 + i.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Multiplication by i rotates anticlockwise π/2 about the origin.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Plots iz₂ at modulus 4, argument π/12.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Inside or on circle |z − i| ≤ 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Correct centre and radius.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Wedge satisfies π/6 ≤ Arg(z) ≤ 5π/6.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Includes boundary rays and the origin where consistent with the shaded region.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>Liquid volume is V(h) = ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>h</sup>e<sup>−x²/100</sup>dx m<sup>3</sup>, 0 ≤ h ≤ 15 metres.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine dV/dh at h = 0.5 m.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Interpret this result.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>Height varies as h(t) = 3t<sup>2</sup> − t + 4, t ≥ 0 seconds. Determine dh/dt when h = 6 m.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>Use the chain rule to determine dV/dt when h = 6 m.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(iii)","text":"<p>Given V at t = 2 is 8.439 m<sup>3</sup>, use increments to estimate V at t = 2.1 seconds.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>FTC gives V′(h) = e<sup>−h²/100</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>V′(0.5) = e<sup>−0.0025</sup> ≈ 0.9975 m<sup>3</sup>/m.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>It is the instantaneous rate of change of volume with respect to height at height 0.5 m.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>Solves 3t<sup>2</sup> − t + 4 = 6: valid t = 1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>h′(1) = 6(1) − 1 = 5 m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>dV/dt = e<sup>−h²/100</sup>(6t − 1).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>At h = 6, t = 1: 5e<sup>−0.36</sup> ≈ 3.488 m<sup>3</sup>/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iii)</td><td>At t = 2, h = 14 and h′ = 11.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iii)</td><td>ΔV ≈ 11e<sup>−1.96</sup>(0.1) ≈ 0.15494 m<sup>3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iii)</td><td>V(2.1) ≈ 8.439 + 0.15494 = 8.594 m<sup>3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2019-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>Let w = (1 − i)/(2√2). Complex number z is shown in the Argand diagram.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Express w = r cis θ, −π &lt; θ ≤ π.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Express z exactly as a + bi.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine exact polar forms of wz and w<sup>2</sup>z.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Plot and label wz and w<sup>2</sup>z on the diagram.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Describe the transformations performed by successive multiplication by w.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Modulus ½ and argument −π/4; w = ½cis(−π/4).</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Reads z = 4cis(2π/3).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>z = −2 + 2√3 i.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>wz = 2cis(5π/12).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>w<sup>2</sup>z = cis(π/6).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Plots wz at radius 2, angle 5π/12.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Plots w<sup>2</sup>z at radius 1, angle π/6.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Dilation about origin with scale factor ½.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Clockwise rotation π/4.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2016-ASSUMED-Q21":{"question":"<p>A lighthouse stands 12 km offshore opposite X. The beam rotates anticlockwise at three revolutions per minute: dθ/dt = 6π rad/min. Its light moves along a shoreline wall, with displacement y from X.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show dy/dθ = 12/cos<sup>2</sup>θ.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the light’s velocity along the wall when it is 5 km north of X. Use dy/dt = (dy/dθ)(dθ/dt).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses tan θ = y/12.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Rearranges y = 12tan θ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Differentiates to 12sec<sup>2</sup>θ = 12/cos<sup>2</sup>θ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>At y = 5, right triangle has sides 5, 12, 13, so sec<sup>2</sup>θ = 169/144.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>dy/dt = 12(169/144)(6π).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Velocity = 169π/2 ≈ 265.46 km/min northward.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2017-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Andrew borrows $14999 for a car after a $1200 deposit. Interest is 1.80% p.a. in year one, then 3.24% p.a., added monthly. Monthly repayments are $420.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Express the first-year repayment process recursively.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>How much is owed after one year?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>How much is owed after two years?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>How long until the loan is repaid?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Determine the final repayment.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Calculate the total cost of the car.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>T₀ = 14999.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>T<sub>n+1</sub> = 1.0015T<sub>n</sub> − 420 for the first 12 months.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>T₁₂ ≈ $10189.43.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses new monthly multiplier 1.0027.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Starts the second year at $10189.43 and makes 12 further repayments.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Balance after two years ≈ $5408.99.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>After 36 repayments balance ≈ $471.35, so one further $420 payment does not clear it.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Repaid at month 38: 3 years 2 months.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>After 37 payments balance ≈ $52.62.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Adds the final month’s interest: last payment ≈ $52.77.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>37 full payments cost $15540.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Adds final payment $52.77.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Adds deposit $1200; total ≈ $16792.77.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2024-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>The W-graph is W<sub>k</sub>(x) = |(k/2)|x − k| − k|, k &gt; 0. Graphs of W₂ and g(x) = |x − 2| − 2 are shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Sketch W₃(x) = |(3/2)|x − 3| − 3|.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>How many solutions does W<sub>k</sub>(x) = k have? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Using the general W-graph, express ∫<sub>k−2</sub><sup>k+2</sup>W<sub>k</sub>(x)dx in terms of k.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Shows intercepts (1, 0), (5, 0), central peak (3, 3) and y-intercept (0, 1.5).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Correct W shape with slopes of magnitude 3/2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Three solutions x = k − 4, k, k + 4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Horizontal line y = k meets the two outer branches and the central peak.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>On [k − 2, k + 2], the region is two triangles.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Each has base 2 and height k.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integral = 2k.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>The proportion of caravans towed by vehicles with incorrect towing capacity is p.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Use calculus to show p̂ = 0.5 maximises margin of error. Since z and n are constants, use E = √[p̂(1 − p̂)].</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>How many vehicles should be tested to estimate p within 8% at 99% confidence?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>A later 99% confidence interval is (0.342, 0.558). Estimate how many sampled vehicles had incorrect towing capacity.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Differentiates E′ = (1 − 2p̂)/(2√[p̂(1 − p̂)]).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Sets derivative zero to obtain p̂ = 0.5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Derivative changes from positive to negative, so this maximises E.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses worst-case p̂ = 0.5 and z ≈ 2.576.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>n ≥ (2.576)<sup>2</sup>(0.25)/(0.08)<sup>2</sup> ≈ 259.21.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Round upward: at least 260 vehicles.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Midpoint gives p̂ = 0.45.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Margin of error = 0.108.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Solves 0.108 = 2.576√(0.45 × 0.55/n), giving n ≈ 141.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Incorrect-capacity count ≈ 0.45n ≈ 63 vehicles (allow rounding from the stated interval).</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2017-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>A project has activities and immediate predecessors:</p><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Predecessors</th><th>Hours</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>—</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>—</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>A</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>A</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>—</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>—</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>B, C</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>H</td><td>D</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>J</td><td>E, F</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>K</td><td>H, J</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>L</td><td>G, K</td><td>6</td></tr></table><p></p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete the project network.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>State the critical path and minimum completion time.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine float time, earliest start and latest start for G.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Either G or J must take three extra hours. Choose the one minimising project completion time and justify.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Adds E and L with correct connections.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Labels at least three other activities correctly.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Completes all activities and precedence connections correctly.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Critical path A–D–H–K–L.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Minimum time = 33 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Float = 2 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Earliest start = 18 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Latest start = 20 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Extra 3 hours on G makes completion 34 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Extra 3 hours on J makes completion 35 hours.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Choose G for the shorter project duration.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>A random sample of n Australian annual incomes has standard deviation 10.98 thousand dollars. Its 99% confidence interval I₁ is 90 ≤ μ ≤ 94.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Calculate n.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Another sample of the same size gives a 99% interval I₂. State two ways the intervals may differ.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>A third sample of 50 gives the widest 99% interval I₃. James says it is more likely to contain μ. Is he correct? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Margin 2 = z(10.98)/√n, with z ≈ 2.576.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>n ≈ 200 (a result of 201 using z = 2.58 and upward rounding is accepted in the source).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Different centres because sample means differ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Different widths because sample standard deviations may differ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>James is not correct.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>All intervals use the same 99% confidence procedure; a wider interval due to smaller sample size does not increase that confidence level.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>An ant colony satisfies dP/dt = 0.01P(100 − P) for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4 days, with population graph shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Use the growth-rate equation to explain the population’s variation on this interval.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>At t = 4 the limiting population increases to 300. Sketch expected population for t &gt; 4.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P grows while below 100, with increasing growth rate until P = 50.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>After P = 50 growth slows as P approaches 100.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Continues from the existing population at t = 4, increasing toward 300.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Shows an inflection at P = 150 and horizontal asymptote P = 300.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":4,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>A colony is in danger below 1000 animals. It has 2300 animals at the start of 2011 and grows as P = 2300e<sup>0.065t</sup>, t years from that date.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the population at the start of 2014 to the nearest 10 animals.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the growth rate at t = 2.5 years.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>From the beginning of 2017 a disease causes continuous decline at 8.25% of population per year. When does the colony become in danger, to the nearest month?</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Substitutes t = 3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Population ≈ 2800 animals.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>P′ = 0.065(2300)e<sup>0.065t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>At t = 2.5, rate ≈ 175.88 animals/year.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Population at start of 2017 = 2300e<sup>0.39</sup> ≈ 3397.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Models later population Q(s) = 2300e<sup>0.39</sup>e<sup>−0.0825s</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Solves Q(s) = 1000, giving s ≈ 14.82 years after start of 2017.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>The colony crosses the threshold around October 2031.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2016-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Therese’s Dublin walking-tour network has vertices for places of interest and edges for direct routes.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Use Euler’s formula to verify the network is connected.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Explain a closed walk.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>State the two properties of a Hamiltonian cycle.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Mark a Hamiltonian cycle starting at G.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Counts v = 11, e = 17, f = 8 including the outer face.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>v + f − e = 11 + 8 − 17 = 2, consistent with the connected planar network.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>A walk starting and ending at the same vertex.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Visits every vertex exactly once except the repeated starting vertex.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Returns to the starting vertex.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>One valid cycle G–D–C–F–B–A–E–H–J–K–I–G.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Correctly marks every edge of that cycle, or its reverse.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2024-ASSUMED-Q18":{"question":"<p>A drone has r(t) = (e<sup>t</sup> + e<sup>−t</sup>, e<sup>t</sup> − e<sup>−t</sup>) metres, 0 ≤ t ≤ 5 seconds. It starts at A and reaches B when t = ln 3.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine the exact position vector of B.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine velocity v(t).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find distance from A to B to 0.001 m.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find its travel direction at t = 2 to 0.1 degrees.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Determine the Cartesian equation of its path.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Substitutes e<sup>ln 3</sup> = 3 and e<sup>−ln 3</sup> = 1/3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>B = (10/3, 8/3).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>x′ = e<sup>t</sup> − e<sup>−t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>y′ = e<sup>t</sup> + e<sup>−t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses distance ∫|v(t)|dt.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Forms ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>ln 3</sup>√(2e<sup>2t</sup> + 2e<sup>−2t</sup>)dt.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Distance ≈ 3.036 m.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>At t = 2 velocity ≈ (7.254, 7.524).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Direction ≈ 46.0° anticlockwise from positive x-axis.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Uses x + y = 2e<sup>t</sup> and x − y = 2e<sup>−t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Multiplies to obtain x<sup>2</sup> − y<sup>2</sup> = 4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Restricts to 2 ≤ x ≤ e<sup>5</sup> + e<sup>−5</sup>, 0 ≤ y ≤ e<sup>5</sup> − e<sup>−5</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2024-FREE-Q07":{"question":"<p>A bicycle rolls down a ramp from height 5.15 m. Its final speed is s(θ) = √[(101sin θ − cos θ)/sin θ] m/second, where θ is the ramp angle in radians.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find speed at θ = 45°.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Differentiate (101sin θ − cos θ)/sin θ, simplifying your answer.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Hence show s′(θ) = [1/(2sin<sup>2</sup>θ)]√[sin θ/(101sin θ − cos θ)].</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Use increments to estimate the speed change from 45° to 46°.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Substitutes sin(π/4) = cos(π/4).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Speed = √100 = 10 m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses quotient rule or rewrites as 101 − cot θ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Simplifies numerator to sin<sup>2</sup>θ + cos<sup>2</sup>θ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Derivative = 1/sin<sup>2</sup>θ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Applies chain rule to the square root.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Simplifies to the required formula.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Angle increment = π/180 radians.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>s′(π/4) = 1/10.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Δs ≈ π/1800 ≈ 0.001745 m/second increase.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-FREE-Q02":{"question":"<p>Women’s heights are normal with mean 163 cm and standard deviation 7 cm. Use the approximate 68%, 95%, 99.7% rule for one, two and three standard deviations.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Comment on the claim that almost all heights are between 142 and 184 cm.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Approximately what percentage are taller than 170 cm?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Approximately 2.5% are shorter than what height?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>142 and 184 are mean ± 3 standard deviations.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>About 99.7% lie in this interval, so almost all is reasonable.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>170 is one standard deviation above the mean.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Upper tail ≈ (100 − 68)/2 = 16%.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Lower 2.5% is about two standard deviations below the mean.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Height = 163 − 14 = 149 cm.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2017-ASSUMED-Q08":{"question":"<p>Ming establishes a $6000 annual student perpetuity, paid as a single amount at the start of each year.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>An account earns 5.9% p.a., compounded monthly. Show that $98974, to the nearest dollar, is required to maintain the perpetuity.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>She has five years to accumulate $98974 using regular quarterly payments to an account earning 5.4% p.a., compounded monthly. Find the quarterly payment if the initial deposit is $1000.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Effective annual rate i = (1 + 0.059/12)<sup>12</sup> − 1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses capital P satisfying Pi = 6000 for each annual payment.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P ≈ $98974, as required under the source’s annual-payment convention.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Equivalent quarterly rate j = (1 + 0.054/12)<sup>3</sup> − 1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Sets 98974 = 1000(1 + j)<sup>20</sup> + R[(1 + j)<sup>20</sup> − 1]/j.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Quarterly payment R ≈ $4283.77.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-FREE-Q01":{"question":"<p>Anastasia’s journey-time histogram is shown. Use its relative frequencies to estimate the probability of her next journey:</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Taking less than 36 minutes.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Taking at least 35 minutes and no more than 39 minutes.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>On three consecutive days she leaves at 9.22 am and must arrive by 10 am. Estimate the probability of arriving on time all three days.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>0.02 + 0.04 + 0.04 = 0.10.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Adds the four relevant interval frequencies.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>0.04 + 0.02 + 0.08 + 0.12 = 0.26.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>A 38-minute journey or less has estimated probability 0.20.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Assuming independent days, probability = 0.20<sup>3</sup> = 0.008.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":5,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2018-FREE-Q08":{"question":"<p>A parallelepiped has sides a = (3, 6, 0), b = (−8, 2, 0), c = (−1, 2, 5), with base OAPB. Let θ be the angle between a and b and ϕ the angle between c and the positive z-axis.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine a × b.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Explain why c · (a × b) gives its volume. You may use |a × b| = |a||b|sin θ and volume = base area × perpendicular height.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine its exact volume.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Correct determinant/cross-product calculation.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>a × b = (0, 0, 54).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Magnitude |a × b| gives the parallelogram base area.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Dotting with c multiplies by the component of c perpendicular to the base, giving area times height (positive for this orientation).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Volume = (−1, 2, 5) · (0, 0, 54) = 270 cubic units.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":5,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2020-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>A tourist walks up a cylindrical tower along r(t) = (10cos(0.1t), 10sin(0.1t), 0.2t) metres, reaching the deck at t = 65π seconds.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine deck height to 0.01 m.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine v(t).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Show that speed is constant and determine it to 0.01 m/second.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Height = 0.2(65π) = 13π ≈ 40.84 m.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Differentiates horizontal components to (−sin(0.1t), cos(0.1t)).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>v(t) = (−sin(0.1t), cos(0.1t), 0.2).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Speed = √[sin<sup>2</sup>(0.1t) + cos<sup>2</sup>(0.1t) + 0.04].</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Trig identity gives √1.04, independent of t.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Constant speed ≈ 1.02 m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2020-FREE-Q07":{"question":"<p>Consider f(x) = e<sup>2x</sup> − 4e<sup>x</sup>.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine all x-intercepts. You may use f(x) = e<sup>x</sup>(e<sup>x</sup> − 4).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show that its only turning point is (ln 2, −4).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine all inflection points.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Sketch f, clearly labelling intercepts, turning point and inflection points. Useful values: ln 1 = 0, ln 2 ≈ 0.7, ln 3 ≈ 1.1, ln 4 ≈ 1.4.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Sets e<sup>x</sup>(e<sup>x</sup> − 4) = 0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>e<sup>x</sup> never equals zero.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Only x-intercept (ln 4, 0).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>f′ = 2e<sup>x</sup>(e<sup>x</sup> − 2).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Only stationary x = ln 2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>f(ln 2) = −4 and derivative changes from negative to positive.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>f″ = 4e<sup>x</sup>(e<sup>x</sup> − 1).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Only zero is x = 0, with a concavity change.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Inflection point (0, −3).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows x-intercept (ln 4, 0).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows minimum (ln 2, −4).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Shows y-intercept/inflection (0, −3).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Correct curve shape and left horizontal asymptote y = 0.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2022-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>A sprinter has velocity v(t) = −10e<sup>−0.8t</sup> − 0.05e<sup>0.2t</sup> + 10.05 m/second, measured from the start of a 100 m race.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine acceleration at t = 3 seconds.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use calculus to find maximum velocity and when it occurs.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine displacement x(t), given x(0) = 0.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Determine the 100 m finishing time.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>a(t) = 8e<sup>−0.8t</sup> − 0.01e<sup>0.2t</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>a(3) ≈ 0.708 m/second<sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Sets a(t) = 0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Obtains e<sup>t</sup> = 800, t = ln 800 ≈ 6.685 seconds.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Confirms maximum through acceleration sign change or negative v″.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Maximum velocity ≈ 9.812 m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integrates to 12.5e<sup>−0.8t</sup> − 0.25e<sup>0.2t</sup> + 10.05t + C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Uses x(0) = 0 to find C = −12.25.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>x(t) = 12.5e<sup>−0.8t</sup> − 0.25e<sup>0.2t</sup> + 10.05t − 12.25.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Solves x(t) = 100 numerically.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Finishing time ≈ 11.413 seconds.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2021-FREE-Q08":{"question":"<p>The heart-shaped figure is x<sup>2</sup> + (y − √|x|)<sup>2</sup> = 2. For x ≥ 0 it becomes x<sup>2</sup> + (y − √x)<sup>2</sup> = 2. The two y-values at each x are equally spaced above and below y = √x.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Explain why the right-half curve has domain 0 ≤ x ≤ √2.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show that its total enclosed area is 4∫<sub>0</sub><sup>√2</sup>√(2 − x<sup>2</sup>)dx.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Using x = √2 sin θ, evaluate this area and explain the reference to π being at the heart of mathematics.</p>","marks":5,"lines":15,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Rearranges (y − √x)<sup>2</sup> = 2 − x<sup>2</sup> ≥ 0.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Together with x ≥ 0 this gives 0 ≤ x ≤ √2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Vertical separation of the branches is 2√(2 − x<sup>2</sup>).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses symmetry about the y-axis to double the right-half area, giving the stated integral.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Substitutes x = √2 sin θ, dx = √2 cos θ dθ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Transforms limits 0 and √2 to 0 and π/2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area = 8∫<sub>0</sub><sup>π/2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>θ dθ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integrates using cos<sup>2</sup>θ = (1 + cos 2θ)/2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area = 2π square units.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2020-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A random sample of n shoppers gives a 95% confidence interval for mean spending μ of $150 to $200. The population standard deviation σ is known.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine the sample mean.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Calculate the standard deviation of the sample mean to 0.01.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>A new sample is twice as large. Find the probability that its sample mean differs from μ by less than $10.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Sample mean = $175.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Confidence margin is $25.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses 25 = 1.96(σ/√n).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Standard deviation of sample mean = 25/1.96 ≈ $12.76.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>New standard error = (25/1.96)/√2 ≈ 9.019.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Standardises the ±$10 bounds to ±1.109.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Probability = Φ(1.109) − Φ(−1.109).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Probability ≈ 0.73.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2019-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>A particle follows r(t) = (−2cos(t/2), 1 − sin t) metres. Its path is shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine and mark its starting point A.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine and illustrate its initial velocity.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Write a trigonometric integral for the distance for one complete circuit. Do not evaluate.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Determine its Cartesian path equation.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>r(0) = (−2, 1).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>v(t) = (sin(t/2), −cos t).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>v(0) = (0, −1) m/second.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Draws a downward vector at A.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>One complete circuit takes 4π seconds.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Speed = √[sin<sup>2</sup>(t/2) + cos<sup>2</sup>t].</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Distance ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>4π</sup>√[sin<sup>2</sup>(t/2) + cos<sup>2</sup>t]dt.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Uses cos(t/2) = −x/2 and sin t = 1 − y.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Identity gives (1 − y)<sup>2</sup> + (x<sup>2</sup>/2 − 1)<sup>2</sup> = 1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Equivalent equation (y − 1)<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> − x<sup>4</sup>/4, −2 ≤ x ≤ 2.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2019-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>Mehmet invests $3600 at 3.65% p.a., compounded monthly, and deposits $250 at each month’s end for three years.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Write a recurrence for the month-end investment value.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Will this double his initial savings in one year? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find total interest received after three years.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>After two years, deposits fall to $120 per month. How much less will the investment be worth at the end of year three?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>T₀ = 3600.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>T<sub>n+1</sub> = (1 + 0.0365/12)T<sub>n</sub> + 250.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Calculates T₁₂ ≈ $6784.32.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>This is below $7200, so initial savings have not doubled.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>T₃₆ ≈ $13511.92.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Total deposits = 3600 + 36(250) = $12600.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Interest = $911.92.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Monthly contribution reduction = $130 for 12 months.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Lost future value = 130[(1 + 0.0365/12)<sup>12</sup> − 1]/(0.0365/12).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Investment is reduced by approximately $1586.36.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2023-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>A building has constant cross-section with roof h(w) = 6sin(w/10) + 3sin(w/5), 0 ≤ w ≤ 10π, in metres. The window lies below the roof and above g(w) = 7cos(w/20).</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine the building’s cross-sectional area.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Determine the window intersections w₁ and w₂.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Determine the window area.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Use calculus to determine maximum roof height.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Area = ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>10π</sup>[6sin(w/10) + 3sin(w/5)]dw.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Area = 120 m<sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Solves h(w) = g(w).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>w₁ ≈ 6.6511 m, w₂ ≈ 18.4122 m.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Sets ∫<sub>w₁</sub><sup>w₂</sup>[h(w) − g(w)]dw.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Window area ≈ 13.94 m<sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>h′(w) = 0.6cos(w/10) + 0.6cos(w/5).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Solves h′ = 0; interior maximum at w = 10π/3.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Verifies maximum with negative second derivative or sign change.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Maximum height = 9√3/2 ≈ 7.79 m.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2019-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>Jake’s park designs use edges for paths and vertices for huts. His drawings are shown.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Redraw the first graph as a connected planar graph with six vertices.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Redraw the second graph as a bipartite graph.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>A complete graph must have 21 paths. How many shelter huts are needed?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>Preserves all six vertices and edge connections.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>Draws without crossing edges; recognising that the original abstract graph is planar is also accepted in the source.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Separates vertices into two sets.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Every edge joins different sets, with all original connections preserved.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>No edge joins two vertices in the same set.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>n(n − 1)/2 = 21 gives n = 7 huts.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2016-ASSUMED-Q07":{"question":"<p>Julie’s $13000 car depreciates by T<sub>n+1</sub> = 0.85T<sub>n</sub>, T₀ = 13000, where n is years.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete values to the nearest dollar.</p><table><tr><th>n</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th></tr><tr><td>Value ($)</td><td>13000</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table><p></p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Determine annual depreciation rate.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Determine an explicit nth-term rule.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the value after eight years to the nearest dollar.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>She sells at the end of the year in which value falls below half the purchase price. After how many years should she sell?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Year 1: $11050.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Years 2 and 3: $9393 and $7984.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>15% per year.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Identifies a geometric sequence.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>T<sub>n</sub> = 13000(0.85)<sup>n</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Substitutes n = 8.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Value ≈ $3542.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Solves 13000(0.85)<sup>n</sup> = 6500, giving n ≈ 4.265.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Sell at the end of year 5.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2022-FREE-Q06":{"question":"<p>Useful values for angles 0, π/6, π/4, π/3, π/2 are:</p><table><tr><th>Angle</th><th>0</th><th>π/6</th><th>π/4</th><th>π/3</th><th>π/2</th></tr><tr><td>sin</td><td>0</td><td>½</td><td>√2/2</td><td>√3/2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>cos</td><td>1</td><td>√3/2</td><td>√2/2</td><td>½</td><td>0</td></tr></table><p></p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Differentiate x sin(πx/4).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Hence show ∫(πx/4)cos(πx/4)dx = x sin(πx/4) + (4/π)cos(πx/4) + c.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Time T in minutes between calls has density p(t) = (π/4)cos(πt/4) for 0 ≤ t ≤ 2, and zero otherwise. Find P(T &lt; 40 seconds) exactly.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Use (a)(ii) to determine expected time between calls.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>Product and chain rules give sin(πx/4) + (πx/4)cos(πx/4).</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Rearranges the derivative identity.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Integrates −sin(πx/4) to (4/π)cos(πx/4).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Obtains the stated antiderivative plus c.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>40 seconds = 2/3 minute.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Integrates ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2/3</sup>(π/4)cos(πt/4)dt = [sin(πt/4)]<sub>0</sub><sup>2/3</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Probability = sin(π/6) = ½.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Uses E(T) = ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>t(π/4)cos(πt/4)dt.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Evaluates [t sin(πt/4) + (4/π)cos(πt/4)]<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>E(T) = 2 − 4/π minutes.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2022-ASSUMED-Q07":{"question":"<p>Ocean-floor displacement is D(x) = (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> + cos(2x − 3π) − 5 for x₁ ≤ x ≤ x₂, and −2 otherwise. x and D are kilometres. The trench lies below the flat seabed.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Determine x₁ and x₂.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Use calculus to find the shaded trench cross-sectional area.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use calculus to find maximum depth below sea level.</p>","marks":5,"lines":15,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>Solves D(x) = −2.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>x₁ ≈ 2.3004, x₂ ≈ 5.9438.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Area = ∫<sub>x₁</sub><sup>x₂</sup>[3 − (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> − cos(2x − 3π)]dx.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Antiderivative 3x − (x − 4)<sup>3</sup>/3 − ½sin(2x − 3π).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Area ≈ 7.029 km<sup>2</sup>.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>D′(x) = 2(x − 4) − 2sin(2x − 3π).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Solves D′ = 0 at x ≈ 3.4392.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>D″(x) = 2 − 4cos(2x − 3π), positive there.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Compares stationary depth with the endpoints to confirm the global minimum.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Maximum depth below sea level ≈ 5.514 km.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2020-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>A directed sewage-pipe network is shown. Edge capacities are litres per minute.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show x = 38 given that the indicated cut has value 57.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Calculate maximum flow.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Mark one valid flow along each pipe attaining that maximum on Diagram 2.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Pipes BD, DC and DT are added with capacities shown. Determine the effect on maximum flow.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Cut capacity is x + 19 = 57.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Hence x = 38.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Paths SBCT carry 19, SAT 22, SACT 12, or uses cut AT + AC + BC.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Maximum flow = 53 L/min.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>One allocation: SA 34, SB 19, AB 0, AC 12, AT 22, BC 19, CT 31.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>All capacities and flow conservation satisfied.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Accounts for the extra routes to T and the new limiting cut.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>New maximum flow = 62 L/min, an increase of 9 L/min.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2019-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>An elevator ascends from ground to sixth floor in 16 seconds with velocity v(t) = (9π/16)sin(πt/16) m/second.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine acceleration and sketch it for 0 ≤ t ≤ 16.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use acceleration to explain velocity on 0 &lt; t &lt; 8 and 8 &lt; t &lt; 16.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Given ground-floor displacement x = 0, determine x(t) and the sixth-floor height.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>a(t) = (9π<sup>2</sup>/256)cos(πt/16).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Sketches decreasing half-cosine from 9π<sup>2</sup>/256 to its negative, crossing zero at t = 8.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>On 0 &lt; t &lt; 8 acceleration is positive, so upward velocity increases.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>On 8 &lt; t &lt; 16 acceleration is negative, so upward velocity decreases.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Maximum velocity occurs at t = 8; elevator remains moving upward until it stops at t = 16.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integrates x(t) = −9cos(πt/16) + C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>x(0) = 0 gives C = 9.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>x(t) = 9[1 − cos(πt/16)]; sixth floor is x(16) = 18 m high.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true}});
